raw=[
 ('01',['FB168-01','FB168-02'],'ðone weg ðe him getǣht wæs','Relative ðe refers to the road; getǣht wæs is a past passive, with him the person shown the route.'),
 ('02',['FB168-02'],'ōð-ðæt hē becōm','Until he arrived: an endpoint clause; retain the source macron.'),
 ('03',['FB168-03','FB168-04'],'hwone hē biddan meahte līfes fultumes','hwone is the person asked, while līfes fultumes names the requested support of life. meahte can be indicative or subjunctive in form; context is a question within his thought, not proof of a uniquely recognizable ending.'),
 ('04',['FB168-04','FB168-05'],'geseah hē ǣnne nacodne cnapan ... iernan','Accusative participant with a perception infinitive. Cook’s glossary allows nacod to mean wearing only a tunic here; do not require a claim of total nudity.'),
 ('05',['FB168-06','FB168-07'],'geongra manna plegan ... tō ðǣm bæðstęde belimpende','plegan literally denotes play/games; carrying equipment for the young men’s games is a contextual explanation, not a new unrestricted dictionary sense. belimpende modifies plegan, as note6 says; it is not another finite action of the boy.'),
 ('06',['FB168-08','FB168-09'],'Gehīere, gē ceasterwaran ...','The call is addressed to the plural audience. Supply the whole source exhortation; do not replace its Gehīere with a silently regularized imperative.'),
 ('07',['FB168-11','FB168-12'],'hine unscrȳdde ðǣm healfan sciccelse ðe hē on hæfde','Reflexive undressing and the garment in the dative; the relative clause says he had the cloak on. Preserve the source construction as a whole.'),
 ('08',['FB168-13'],'hiera ānra gehwilcne on hiera weorce','Each single one of them at their work: first hiera participates in the partitive expression; second is possessive. Keep the printed short spelling.'),
 ('09',['FB169-02','FB169-03'],'mid swiftre hrædnesse geslægene','Cook note2 remarks on the participle’s awkward form. Supply the complete phrase as describing the ball struck with swift speed; retain geslægene rather than silently repairing agreement.'),
 ('10',['FB169-05','FB169-06','FB169-07'],'oncnēow ... ðæt hē wiste ðæt hē næfde his gelīcan','The nested reference is unclear, as Cook note6 states. næfde is negative: the text does not say he had an equal. The Latin comparison concerns the king’s previously unmatched skill; use it as help while retaining the OE uncertainty, not as an invisible replacement text.'),
 ('11',['FB169-08'],'ðæs-ðe mē ðyncð','As it seems to me: exact parenthetic impersonal construction, dative experiencer mē.'),
 ('12',['FB169-11','FB169-12','FB169-13'],'swang ... ðone top ... swilce ... gewęnd wǣre','Retain the OE’s throwing/striking of the top, tentatively a ball. Cook’s Latin comparison means rubbing him with ointment and the rejuvenating effect. No shoulder/neck noun occurs. Learners need this English explanation, not independent Latin translation. wǣre belongs to the imagined transformation comparison.'),
 ('13',['FB169-12'],'ðǣm cyninge wæs geðūht','It seemed to the king: whole impersonal be-plus-participle construction supplied; do not read it as an ordinary king-as-subject clause.'),
 ('14',['FB169-14','FB169-15','FB169-16'],'him ... hē hine lǣdde be ðǣre handa ... him ... gewęnde','Keep the original notes11,14–16 available for the pronoun chain. The later him is reflexive; the by-the-hand phrase specifies how one person leads another.'),
 ('15',['FB169-16','FB169-17'],'ðæs weges ðe hē ǣr cōm','Source genitive route expression, followed by relative ðe. Compare the bounded genitive-route teaching and Cook35.3 model Gā ðæs weges ðe ðū cōme; retain this exact source phrasing with its past-time reference.'),
 ('16',['FB170-01','FB170-02'],'siððan Apollonius āgān wæs','After Apollonius had gone away: intransitive completed departure with be, not a passive in which someone sends him away.'),
 ('17',['FB170-02'],'ðurh ðā gemǣnan hǣle','Oath by the common safety/salvation; welfare is an explanatory contextual alternative. hǣle is feminine accusative singular of hǣlu, reusing the older exact source form and safety/rescue sense. No possessive our is printed.'),
 ('18',['FB170-04','FB170-05','FB170-06'],'ðurh hwilces geonges mannes ðēnunge ... hwæt se geonga mann sīe','The service is the means of the benefit; hwilces ... mannes is its genitive dependent. hwæt asks what sort/status of man he is. sīe is the dependent interrogative be form.'),
 ('19',['FB170-08','FB170-09','FB170-10','FB170-11'],'ēode æfter Apollonio ... ðe ðū æfter āscodest','Going after a person, then asking about him: the relative marker is the complement of æfter within the clause; do not force temporal after for either use.'),
 ('20',['FB170-13'],'Ðēah hē hit self forswīge','Concessive clause: even if he himself keeps it unspoken. Supply the source forswīge with its present subjunctive interpretation; do not demand a full new weak-verb paradigm.'),
 ('21',['FB170-14','FB170-15','FB170-16'],'sęge him ðæt ‘se cyning bitt ðē ðæt ðū cume ...’','Cook note15 explicitly diagnoses mixed direct/reported construction. First him is the messenger’s addressee; within the quoted message ðē is the person invited (accusative), and ðū/cume address him directly. Retain wording and speech levels; no silent repair.')]
